# Privacy Policy

**Last updated: 14 August 2026**

This Privacy Policy describes how **Andoware** handles information when you use **Offboarding Auditor for Jira**.

Offboarding Auditor for Jira is an Atlassian Forge app designed to help Jira administrators identify Jira assets and responsibilities that remain associated with inactive or deleted user accounts.

## Data We Process

To perform an audit, the app may process information available through your Jira site, including:

- Atlassian account IDs
- User display names
- User active or inactive status
- User avatar URLs when searching for an active user during a handover
- Saved filter names and metadata
- Dashboard names and metadata
- Board names and metadata
- Project names, keys and lead information
- Component names and lead information
- Sharing, favourite and related asset metadata required to display audit results

Jira may return an email address as part of some user information. Offboarding Auditor for Jira does not intentionally display, persist or export email addresses.

The app does **not** access or store Jira issue contents or saved-filter JQL for the purposes of its audit.

## How We Use Data

The app processes Jira data only as necessary to provide its functionality, including:

- Identifying inactive or deleted Jira accounts
- Identifying filters and dashboards associated with those accounts
- Identifying boards that depend on affected filters
- Identifying projects and components with inactive leads
- Displaying audit results to Jira administrators
- Supporting administrator-confirmed filter ownership handover
- Verifying guided dashboard handovers
- Generating CSV exports requested by the administrator
- Saving limited audit history within the app

The app does not use customer data for advertising, profiling or marketing.

## Data Storage

Offboarding Auditor for Jira is built using the Atlassian Forge platform.

The app does not operate its own external application servers or databases for customer Jira data.

Limited information may be stored using Atlassian Forge-hosted storage, including:

- Saved audit history
- Asset names and metadata included in saved audit results
- Relevant account IDs and display names associated with affected assets
- Audit timestamps and summary counts
- The app's onboarding-completed setting

The app keeps a maximum of **25 saved audit records per Jira installation**. When this limit is exceeded, the oldest saved record is removed.

Jira administrators can also delete individual saved audit records or clear saved audit history from within the app.

Forge-hosted data is stored and processed using Atlassian's infrastructure and supported data-residency mechanisms. The location of Forge-hosted data may depend on the customer's Atlassian data-residency configuration and Atlassian's supported hosting locations.

## External Data Transfers

Offboarding Auditor for Jira does **not** send Jira customer data to servers operated by Andoware.

The app communicates with Atlassian Jira APIs and Atlassian Forge services in order to provide its functionality.

The app does not include third-party analytics, advertising trackers or telemetry services.

## Data Sharing

Andoware does not sell customer or personal data.

The app does not share Jira customer data with advertising networks, data brokers or unrelated third parties.

Data is processed through Atlassian services as necessary for the app to operate on the Atlassian Forge platform.

## CSV Exports

When a Jira administrator chooses to export audit results, the CSV file is generated for that administrator from the current audit data.

CSV exports may contain information such as:

- Asset names and IDs
- User display names
- Atlassian account IDs
- Project or filter information
- Audit timestamps and coverage information

Email addresses, Jira issue contents and saved-filter JQL are not intentionally included in CSV exports.

Once a CSV file has been exported, responsibility for storing, sharing and deleting that downloaded file belongs to the customer or administrator who created it.

## App Permissions and Changes to Jira

Running an audit is read-only with respect to Jira data.

Some guided handover features may make a change to Jira only after an administrator explicitly confirms the action. For example, an administrator may choose to reassign ownership of an affected saved filter to an active Jira user.

The app does not automatically perform bulk ownership changes or modify Jira assets simply because an audit has been run.

## Security

Offboarding Auditor for Jira relies on Atlassian Forge for application execution, authentication and access to Jira APIs.

Jira API operations are performed using the permissions granted to the app and the permissions of the Jira user performing the relevant action.

The app does not receive or store Atlassian passwords or access tokens directly.

## Data Retention and Deletion

Saved audit history is retained within Forge-hosted storage until:

- it is deleted by a Jira administrator,
- all saved history is cleared by an administrator, or
- it is removed automatically as part of the app's 25-record history limit.

Other Jira information retrieved during a live audit is used to produce the audit result and is not intentionally persisted unless it forms part of a saved audit-history record.

